.TH DUMPAPPLE 1 2026-10-25 "dumpapple 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dumpapple \- Apple II / IIgs (6502, 65C02, 65816) disassembler producing
byte-identical ca65 listings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dumpapple
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B dumpapple
.RB [ \-h " | " \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dumpapple
loads an Apple IIgs OMF v1/v2 load file (relocating it to a chosen base) or a
raw
binary, follows control flow from the entry points while tracking the M, X and E
flags, and writes a ca65 source listing plus an ld65 configuration that rebuild
the loaded image byte-for-byte.  Without arguments it prints usage and exits 0.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-omf
Force an OMF v1/v2 load file (default: autodetect).
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Force a raw binary.
.TP
.BI \-\-org " ADDR"
Raw load address (default $2000).  The address is also an entry point unless
.B \-\-no\-org\-entry
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-base " ADDR"
OMF: base of the first relocatable segment (default $020000).
.TP
.BI \-\-stride " N"
OMF: alignment between segment bases (default $10000; 0 is treated as 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " CPU"
6502, 65c02 (aliases w65c02, r65c02: the WDC W65C02S, with RMB/SMB/BBR/BBS
and WAI/STP; only early non-Rockwell 65C02s such as Apple's enhanced IIe/IIc
run $x7/$xF as NOPs) or 65816 (aliases 65c816, w65c816); CPU names are
case-insensitive.  With 6502 or 65c02 the image must lie in $0000-$FFFF
(exit 1 otherwise).  Default: 65816 for OMF.  For raw input the CPU is chosen
from flow:
the file is decoded from the entry as a 65816 in emulation mode, and 65816 is
picked when that flow runs a 65816-only instruction (brl, jsl, jml, rtl,
rep, sep, clc;xce, phb/plb, phd/pld, phk, tcd/tdc, tcs/tsc, txy/tyx, xba,
mvn/mvp, pea/pei/per, jsr (abs,x)); otherwise 65c02.  One of brl, jsl, jml, rtl,
rep, sep or clc;xce is enough; the others need at least three, and none counts
inside a run of text bytes.  A hit counts only where a 65C02 decode of the
same flow reaches the same address as an instruction start (falling into it,
at an entry, or via a jump table that decode uses); a decode that slips after
BBR/BBS
or $x3, a jump into an operand, or data the 65C02 decode never reaches is not
evidence, nor is anything after a brk or a non-65816 undefined opcode.  After a
weak first hit, a key counts only if the weak hit is not among text and the
65816 reading runs from it to the key and 4 instructions on without brk/cop/wdm
(immediates sized by m/x tracked through rep/sep, which widen only in native
mode;
carry and E are tracked through clc/sec/xce and every carry-writing instruction,
both starting unknown (E starts in emulation with
.BR "\-\-platform prodos8" ,
as ProDOS 8 launches SYS/BIN files that way); after xce of unknown mode or
plp outside emulation a width is unknown and either size may pass, at most 4
times on a path, so a fifth such
immediate can only make it miss 65816 code).
Where the decodes part at a $xF long instruction (BBR/BBS on a 65C02), a key
within 8 instructions on the 65816 reading counts only if the 65C02 reading
breaks within 8 instructions (brk, undefined opcode, branch off the image or
into an
instruction); a valid 65C02 reading vouches nothing.  COP/WDM, xce with an
unknown carry, long or stack-relative operands alone, and bytes off the flow
are not evidence.  The listing header states the choice as
.B ; cpu: CPU (auto: reason)
and --cpu overrides it.  Selects the decoder and the ca65 .setcpu.
.TP
.BI \-\-entry " ADDR[:STATE]"
Add an entry point, optionally with its own width state; repeatable.  ADDR is
$hex, 0xhex, hex, or SEG:OFF for OMF.
.TP
.BI \-\-platform " P"
none, prodos8 or gsos (default: gsos for OMF, none for raw).  gsos: JSL $E100A8
is followed by 6 inline bytes (call number, parameter block pointer); prodos8:
JSR $BF00 by 3 (command, parameter list pointer).  Each rule that fires is
commented in the listing.  gsos with \-\-cpu 6502 or 65c02 prints a warning,
as GS/OS calls are 65816 JSLs and the rule cannot fire.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tables
Do not follow jump tables or push-address+RTS/RTL dispatch.
.TP
.BI \-\-hints\-lib " NAME"
Also read the shipped hint file NAME.hints (from $DUMPAPPLE_HINTS, hints/
next to the binary, or /usr/local/share/dumpapple/hints), e.g. davex.  Never
applied
automatically.  \-\-hints may also be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-inline
Do not detect routines that read a terminated string after their call.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-imm\-pointers
Do not treat lda #<x / ldx|ldy #>x pairs as code pointers.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " S"
Entry width state: e, m8x8, m8x16, m16x8, m16x16.  Default e for raw input,
m16x16 for OMF.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-smc
Stores with unknown targets may modify code; decoding stops there.  CPU
auto-detect ignores it.
.TP
.B \-\-seg\-entries
OMF: also seed the ENTRY of every other static code segment.  Default: only the
first static code segment's ENTRY (as the GS/OS loader); data, jump-table,
DP/stack and dynamic (KIND $8000) segments never seed code.
This is a heuristic: a code-typed segment may hold data, and its ENTRY is then
decoded anyway (in one real file it produces a false
.B rts
at a music segment's entry).  The default is the reliable output.
Independently of this option, every load-file-1 entry of a KIND $02 jump-table
segment (8-byte header, then 14-byte entries: user ID, load-file, load-segment,
offset, JSL to loader; load-file 0 ends) is an entry point, dynamic segments
included.  The table is listed as data with each entry labelled
.BR jt_ADDR ;
~ExpressLoad is never code.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-seg\-entries
OMF: seed only the first static code segment's ENTRY (default; cancels
.BR \-\-seg\-entries ).
.TP
.BI \-\-hints " FILE"
Hints file (also holds name directives), see below; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write the listing there.  If FILE ends in .s the ld65 config is written next
to it with .cfg replacing .s; otherwise no config is written unless
.B \-\-cfg
is given (so \-o /dev/null works).  Default: listing to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg " FILE"
Write the ld65 config to FILE.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-image " FILE"
Write the loaded, relocated image.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Assemble with ca65, link with ld65 and compare with the image; exit status 3
if the rebuild fails to assemble or link or differs.  The work directory
/tmp/dumpapple\-verify\-XXXXXX is then kept for inspection.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca65 " PATH"
Assembler used by \-\-verify (default ca65 from PATH).
.TP
.BI \-\-ld65 " PATH"
Linker used by \-\-verify (default ld65 from PATH).
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0 (also with no arguments).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit 0.
.SH HINTS FILE
One directive per line; text after # is a comment.
.nf
  entry   ADDR [STATE]     code entry point
  state   ADDR STATE       force width state when flow reaches ADDR
  returns ADDR STATE       calls to ADDR return in STATE
  noreturn ADDR            calls to ADDR (or the call at ADDR) do not return
  region  FILEOFF LEN ADDR raw: list file bytes FILEOFF.. at ADDR (run address)
  data    ADDR LEN         never decode as code
  word    ADDR LEN         data emitted as .word
  name    ADDR NAME        label name
  comment ADDR TEXT        comment line before ADDR
  segbase SEGNUM ADDR      OMF: load segment SEGNUM at ADDR
  table   ADDR COUNT [word|long|rts] [STATE]
                           jump table, overrides the heuristic; without
                           STATE the state at its jump site is used
  inline  ADDR N           jsr/jsl to ADDR is followed by N inline bytes
  inline  ADDR z|hi|lo|len ... by a string ending at a zero byte / bit 7
                           set / bit 7 clear, or led by its length byte
  rtstable ADDR N [STATE]  rts dispatch table: N words, each target-1
  rtstable split HI LO N [STATE]
                           split rts table: N high bytes at HI, low at LO
  (returns ADDR STATE on a BRK address lets flow continue after that BRK)
.fi
ADDR accepts $hex, 0xhex or bare hex, and SEG:OFF for OMF.  STATE is one of
e, m8x8, m8x16, m16x8, m16x16.
.SH JUMP TABLES
jmp (abs,x), jsr (abs,x), jmp (abs), jml [abs] and push-address-minus-1 +
RTS/RTL sequences are followed.  Table length is bounded heuristically (valid
in-image targets that decode, stopping at labels, code, other tables or a
change in the OMF relocation pattern); each table is commented with its origin
and stop reason.  Auto tables are re-bounded to a fixpoint when code or
references found later reach into them.  COP continues after its signature
byte; BRK ends flow.  For OMF input, an instruction whose bytes carry a
relocated field that is not wholly inside its operand bytes is treated as data.
.SH WIDTH TRACKING
REP/SEP, CLC/SEC+XCE and PHP/PLP update the state.  Conflicting states at a
join point make the flag unknown; an immediate whose size depends on an unknown
flag stops decoding there (the bytes are listed as data with a comment) rather
than guessing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (also no arguments, \-\-help, \-\-version).
.TP
.B 1
Cannot read the input or a hints file, bad OMF, empty input, image out of
range, write error, \-\-hints\-lib file not found.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown option, missing or bad argument, more than one FILE,
options without FILE.
.TP
.B 3
\-\-verify failed: the rebuild did not assemble or link, or differs (the work
directory /tmp/dumpapple\-verify\-XXXXXX is kept).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DUMPAPPLE_HINTS
First directory searched by \-\-hints\-lib.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <bindir>/hints
Shipped hint files next to the binary (found via /proc/self/exe).
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/dumpapple/hints
Installed hint files.
.TP
.I /tmp/dumpapple\-verify\-*
Work directories of failed \-\-verify runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
  dumpapple PROG.SYS16 \-o prog.s \-\-verify
  dumpapple \-\-org 2000 \-\-cpu 65816 \-\-state e code.bin \-o code.s
  dumpapple \-\-org 0800 \-\-cpu 6502 apple2.bin \-o a2.s
  dumpapple \-\-platform prodos8 \-\-org 2000 \-\-verify PROG.SYSTEM
.fi
.SH REBUILD
.nf
  ca65 \-\-cpu 65816 \-t none \-o prog.o prog.s
  ld65 \-C prog.cfg \-o prog.bin prog.o
.fi
.SH BUGS
Rockwell bit-op code followed directly by bytes that also read as valid 65816
code may mislead CPU auto-detect; use \-\-cpu.
6502 code built on purpose to look like 65816 (weak opcodes on agreed boundaries
running cleanly into a rep or sep) still picks 65816; use \-\-cpu.
Report bugs at https://github.com/EdgeOfAssembly/dumpapple/issues.
.SH AUTHORS
EdgeOfAssembly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ca65 (1),
.BR ld65 (1),
.BR da65 (1),
.UR https://github.com/EdgeOfAssembly/dumpexe
dumpexe
.UE
